  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nA low level anticyclone tracking east through Ontario and the \nnorthern Great Lakes will funnel drier, less humid air into the area \ntoday into this evening. Active pattern with several rounds of rain \nand embedded storms then return later tonight through the weekend \nas another baroclinic zone and unseasonably moist 2 inch PWAT axis \nset up over the area. A parade of convectively enhanced shortwaves\nand broad WAA/isentropic upglide into the frontal zone will no doubt\naid in the expansion of training elevated convective episodes and\ntorrential rainfall.\n\nA model consensus favors Saturday morning into early Saturday \nafternoon for the first round of elevated convection on the nose of \na ~30 knot low level jet. Higher QPF/PoPs are favored west of I-69\nand north of US 24 with this first round as activity may tend to\noutrun more focused low level convergence with southeastward \nprogression. \n\nA brief lull remains possible in between mid level impulses Saturday \nafternoon-early evening before additional bouts of more organized \nrain/thunder settle in Saturday night into Sunday. This occurs as a \nweak sfc frontal wave aids in enhancing the fgen response. Heavier, \ntraining convective elements remain a concern for localized flooding \ngiven the frontal forcing and moisture rich air mass. A Flood Watch \nmay be needed for portions of the area at some point, but held off \nfor now per collaboration and lingering model spread on timing and \nlocation. Prospects for any isolated severe storms are on the low \nside south of US 30 otherwise later Saturday afternoon-evening as \nthe sfc boundary and potential for any surface based convection \nstruggles to reach this far north. \n\nCooler weather remains in store for next week as the primary Western \nCONUS upper trough releases east, potentially closing off over the \nGreat Lakes by midweek. Continued moist isentropic upglide over \nthe southward sinking frontal zone keep chances for showers through \nat least Monday, with a trend toward drier weather thereafter as the \nleading moisture channel shifts off to the southeast.",
